  <h1>DeepInfer open-sourced <span class="accent">— what DSH / V4.1-Flash installers should know</span></h1>
  <p class="muted small" style="margin-bottom:20px">October 20, 2026 · dshbase · hardware, open gaps, and when the official API is still the right call</p>

  <p>On October 20, 2026, <a href="https://x.com/deepseek_ai/status/2112476496283775135" rel="noopener noreferrer">@deepseek_ai</a> posted a four-part thread introducing <strong>DeepInfer</strong> — DeepSeek’s in-house inference framework — as fully open source, with the code at <a href="https://github.com/deepseek-ai/DeepInfer" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/deepseek-ai/DeepInfer</a>. The pitch is blunt: the same stack that powers <strong>DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash</strong> at production scale is now available so the community can chase lower compute cost and energy use.</p>
  <p>If you came here from <a href={homeUrl}>dshbase</a> because you run <code>npx @deepseek-ai/dsh web</code>, this post is not “go clone the repo tonight.” DeepInfer is a <em>model serving</em> layer. DeepSeek Harness (DSH) is an <em>agent runtime</em>. Confusing the two is how people burn a weekend and still have no agent loop. Below: verified facts, the hardware gate, what the open release deliberately leaves out, how that compares to staying on the official API with DSH, and a short action checklist.</p>

  <h2>What DeepInfer is — and what it is not</h2>
  <p>The official README states it plainly: DeepInfer is the high-performance inference framework that powers DeepSeek’s large-scale production services. The open-source release “provides <strong>offline inference</strong> with the core features needed for serving at scale.” Supported model at ship time: <strong>DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash</strong> (weights pointed at Hugging Face <code>deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017</code>).</p>
  <p>DeepSeek Harness is a different box. DSH is the TypeScript/Node agent surface — tools, plugins, sandboxes, session logs, modes — that talks to a model endpoint. It does not replace an inference engine, and an inference engine does not give you plugins or trajectory tracing. Same company brand, two layers:</p>
  <table class="ztable">
    <tr><th>Layer</th><th>DeepSeek Harness (DSH)</th><th>DeepInfer</th></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Job</strong></td><td>Agent runtime / harness</td><td>Model serving / inference engine</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Typical install</strong></td><td><code>npx @deepseek-ai/dsh web</code></td><td>Build from source (Python 3.12 + CUDA 13 or CANN 9.2)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Talks to</strong></td><td>HTTP APIs (official DeepSeek, OpenRouter, …)</td><td>GPU/NPU kernels + checkpoints</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Gives you</strong></td><td>Tools, plugins, modes, session audit</td><td>Prefill/decode throughput on listed hardware</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Does not give you</strong></td><td>A self-hosted Flash cluster</td><td>Agent loops, plugin market, web UI</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Repo (verify)</strong></td><td>DeepSeek Harness / <code>@deepseek-ai/dsh</code></td><td><a href="https://github.com/deepseek-ai/DeepInfer" rel="noopener noreferrer">deepseek-ai/DeepInfer</a></td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>If your goal is “run an agent on V4.1 Flash,” the default path is still <strong>DSH + official API</strong>. DeepInfer is for teams that already own (or can buy) the listed accelerators and want to operate the serving kitchen themselves.</p>

